# Row B11: lifecycle (x/apibench/b11). See hack/bakeoff.sh.
PKG=./x/apibench/b11
BENCH=BenchmarkAPI/Lifecycle
REF=shutdown
AUTO_KEY=b11
# the benchmark configuration: this row is one of the MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE API shapes.
# Only one shape can be the API, so a shape the API did not take is a loser
# in §9.4(5)'s sense and cannot be retained as a profile the way a §9.1 arm
# can.
CLASS=B
#
# `git worktree add <path> d802b9e4e8c9` restores every shape this row
# measured, which is what makes a re-ratification on a pinned host still
# possible.
REMOVED=retired
ADR=6
TITLE="Lifecycle: Close(), Close(ctx), or Close() plus Shutdown(ctx) (B11)"
# A stop happens once per handle, so BenchmarkAPI/Lifecycle is not the reason a
# shape would be chosen; it exists so that an expensive stop is visible.
# the benchmark configuration's evidence is "cancellation/chaos behaviour; usability",
# and it is three things.
#
# The line counts, per stop, measured over each shape's own lifecycle surface
# (x/apibench/b11/surface.go) so that a call site can only write what its
# shape really has: a graceful stop is one line for the two shapes that have a
# graceful call and nine for the one-method shape, whose caller writes the
# bounded wait itself; a PROMPT stop is one line for the two shapes that kept a
# context-free Close and six for the one-method-with-a-context shape, whose
# caller has to build a context that is already done and then decide that the
# cancellation it asked for is not a failure.
#
# That prompt column is what separates closectx from shutdown. Until the
# surfaces existed all three kinds were one Go type with one method set, both
# shapes' call sites were the identical facade call, and the only number
# between them was the declared Traits.SeparateGracefulCall — which no test
# could refuse in either direction (adr/ADR-017-assumptions.md,
# assumption A23.15).
#
# The chaos tests: a graceful stop refuses publications and new fetches from
# the moment it begins and waits for the handlers already inside, while a
# prompt stop returns with a handler still held. Every stop of every shape is
# raced against a publish and against an outstanding consume for 3000 rounds
# under -race with both halves of the ownership ledger checked every round.
#
# And the row's sharpest finding: the one-method shape's caller-side wait can
# only poll the engine's lease count, which is zero for a fetch parked on an
# empty lane, so it closes at once and reports success — it is PROMPT while
# believing itself graceful. The two shapes with a handle-level counter see the
# parked fetch and pay §4.11's whole drain timeout, because nothing but the
# close can release it. Both branches are asserted
# (adr/ADR-017-assumptions.md).

#
# CRITERION is the benchmark configuration's decision column for this row, quoted.
# MATRIX is the IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX grid the campaign measures the family at.
# DECIDED_AT names the benchmark-name segments the criterion is read at, and
# DECIDED_ON the metrics; `make bench-lint` lists the family's benchmarks at
# the declared grid and fails when a term or a metric is missing from the rows
# every kind has in common, which is the only data `bakeoff decide` reads.
CRITERION="'Close()' + 'Shutdown(ctx)' (cancellation/chaos behaviour; usability)"
# MATRIX=default is the only grid: x/apibench/b11 has no IQMQ_BENCH_MATRIX
# branch, and its four rows per kind are the prompt and the graceful shutdown
# from an idle and a loaded state.
MATRIX=default
DECIDED_AT="prompt graceful state=idle state=ready"
DECIDED_ON="ns/op allocs/op"
